# Object `pattern` is a regular expression compiled from its `body` and
# `flags`, the object `string.regex.pattern`. Its receiver is the `regex`
# it belongs to. Both the parser of the body and the matcher of a text
# live here, together with the helpers they share, such as `any`,
# `member`, or `sequence`. The helpers that two or more others use are
# private. A helper with only one user stays public, since moving it into
# that user would nest the recursive parser deeper than the linter allows,
# and a test here calls it directly through a `pattern` instance, such as
# `"/x/".regex.pattern "x".as-bytes --`. The tests that build a `pattern`
# directly live here, while the tests that go through `compile` and `match`
# live in `regex.eo`. Only `flag` and `worded` stay in `regex`, because
# `compile` needs them too, so `pattern` reads them through its receiver.

